## Connection 1 — Authentication Service (Task 4)
 
**Plain words:** right now, Document Service treats every single request as
if it came from the same one made-up person. Going live means actually
checking who's asking.
 
**What real integration requires** (confirmed from Authentication's real
contract):
 
1. Fetch Authentication's public signing keys from its JWKS endpoint
   (`/.well-known/jwks.json`) and verify the token's signature.
2. Check the token hasn't expired (`exp`) and came from the right issuer
   (`iss`).
3. Read `tenantId` and `sub` (the user ID) from the token — **`tenantId` is
   the confirmed real field name**, not `organization_id`.
4. **Check Redis** for a revocation marker. A token can look perfectly
   valid and still have been "returned" (logged out) — like a movie ticket
   that still looks fine but was already refunded. Skipping this check
   means a logged-out session keeps working.
**New things this adds to the service that don't exist today:** a Redis
connection (new container in `docker-compose.yml`, new client library), and
a JWKS-fetching library. This is the one connection that's genuinely bigger
than a one-line swap — budget real time for it.
 
