## Connection 2 — RBAC Service (Task 5)
 
**Plain words:** even if we know *who* someone is, we still need to know if
they're *allowed* to upload or read documents.
 
**The honest status:** this one can't be fully planned yet. RBAC's own
documentation is explicit that its real shape — a REST call or a shared
code library, and what the request/response even looks like — hasn't been
decided by the team. Document Service is specifically named as one of the
services blocked by this gap.
 
**What to do in the meantime:** nothing more than what's already there.
`permissionClient.js`'s one function, `checkPermission(userId, code)`, is
exactly the shape any future real version will plug into. The permission
codes already chosen (`DOCUMENT_READ`, `DOCUMENT_UPLOAD`, `DOCUMENT_MANAGE`)
follow the `{RESOURCE}_{ACTION}` naming RBAC's own docs confirm — so even
though the mechanism is unknown, the vocabulary is already aligned.
 
**One thing worth flagging to RBAC's owner, before they write the real
contract:** RBAC sits on "the hot path of nearly every write" per its own
docs. If every single document upload/read has to make a slow network call
just to check a permission, that becomes everyone's performance problem, not
just RBAC's. Worth asking whether some form of short-lived caching is part
of the plan.
 
