## Connection 5 — Everyone who listens to Document Service's events
 
**Plain words:** once a document uploads successfully, Document Service
doesn't personally tell the scanner, the search index, or the audit log —
it writes one event, and whoever's listening grabs it on their own
schedule.
 
| Who's listening | Why |
|---|---|
| **Audit** | Universal subscriber — logs that the upload happened |
| **Virus Scanner Worker** | Needs to know a new file exists so it can scan it |
| **OCR Worker** | Pulls text out of the file for search (optional feature) |
| **Metadata Processor** | Enriches the document record further |
| **Search Indexer** | Makes the document findable in search |
 
**What Document Service has to keep stable for all of them:** the exact
shape of the `DocumentUploaded` event — `eventType`, `tenantId`,
`resourceType`, and the `metadata` block (`documentId`, `caseId`,
`storageKey`). Changing any of these field names breaks every listener at
once, so treat this event shape the same way you'd treat a public API
contract, not an internal implementation detail.
 
**The one event Document Service doesn't produce yet:**
`DocumentScanned` — published once a real virus scanner reports a result.
Right now every document sits at `scan_status: PENDING` forever, since
nothing produces that event. This is correctly out of scope until the
scanner itself exists — nothing to fix today, just something to remember.
 
